The provided texts do not say whether a “graphometric signature” is an advanced electronic signature. The context gives the legal requirements an electronic signature must meet to be an advanced electronic signature, but does not classify specific technologies or methods (such as graphometric signatures).

To determine whether a graphometric signature would qualify, it would need to meet the requirements for an advanced electronic signature listed in the Regulation:
- it is uniquely linked to the signatory (E Art. 26(a));
- it is capable of identifying the signatory (E Art. 26(b));
- it is created using electronic signature creation data that the signatory can, with a high level of confidence, use under his sole control (E Art. 26(c));
- it is linked to the data signed therewith so that any subsequent change in the data is detectable (E Art. 26(d)).

(Recitals note that electronic signatures of lower security assurance should also be accepted and that legal effect should not be denied solely because a signature is electronic, but they do not classify specific signature types) (E Rec. 48, E Rec. 49).

- The controller must appoint/use only processors who provide sufficient guarantees to implement appropriate technical and organisational measures so that processing meets the Regulation’s requirements and protects data subjects’ rights (G Art. 28.1).

- The processing carried out by a processor must be governed by a contract or other legal act under Union or Member State law, binding the processor to the controller and setting out the subject‑matter and duration of the processing, the nature and purposes of the processing, the type of personal data and categories of data subjects, taking into account the processor’s specific tasks/responsibilities and the risks to data‑subjects; the parties may use an individual contract or standard contractual clauses (G Rec. 81).

- The processor may not engage another processor (sub‑processor) without the controller’s prior specific or general written authorisation; if general written authorisation is given, the processor must inform the controller of intended additions/replacements so the controller can object (G Art. 28.2).

- Where a processor engages another processor, the same data‑protection obligations as in the contract between controller and processor must be imposed on that other processor (G Art. 28.4).

- The contract must, in particular, require that the processor:
  - processes personal data only on documented instructions from the controller, including as regards transfers to third countries or international organisations (unless required by applicable law, in which case the processor must inform the controller unless prohibited) (G Art. 28.3.a);
  - ensures persons authorised to process the personal data have committed to confidentiality or are under an appropriate statutory obligation of confidentiality (G Art. 28.3.b);
  - takes all measures required pursuant to Article 32 (G Art. 28.3.c);
  - respects the conditions referred to in paragraph 2 (G Art. 28.3.d).

- The provided context for the remainder of the contract requirements (subsections after G Art. 28.3.d) is incomplete; I cannot state those parts beyond what is quoted above from the supplied passages.

- Disputes under a contract are determined by the law governing the contract; where the parties have not chosen the law, “the applicable law should be determined in accordance with the rule specified for the particular type of contract” (RI Rec. 19).

- Where no choice of law has been made, the law governing the contract is determined by the default rules in Article 4:
  - a contract for the sale of goods → law of the country where the seller has his habitual residence (RI Art. 4(a));
  - a contract for the provision of services → law of the country where the service provider has his habitual residence (RI Art. 4(b));
  - a contract relating to a right in rem in immovable property or to a tenancy of immovable property → law of the country where the property is situated (RI Art. 4(c));
  - the provision about tenancies of immovable property concluded for temporary private use for no more than six consecutive months is truncated in the provided context (RI Art. 4(d) — remainder missing).

- Pre‑contractual non‑contractual obligations (culpa in contrahendo) are governed by the law that applies to the contract, or would have applied had the contract been concluded; if that cannot be determined, RII provides fallback rules (RII Art. 12.1; RII Art. 12.2).

- Parties’ autonomy to determine the courts having jurisdiction (for most contracts other than insurance, consumer or employment) is to be respected subject to the Regulation’s exclusive grounds of jurisdiction (B Rec. 19).

- These Regulations govern contracts (agreements between parties) — see Recital 19 (B Rec. 19) which refers to "the autonomy of the parties to a contract".

- However, certain agreements/subjects are excluded from the scope:
  - arbitration agreements and agreements on the choice of court (RI Art. 1 (e));
  - questions involving status or legal capacity of natural persons, obligations from family relationships, matrimonial property regimes, wills and succession, negotiable instruments, etc. (RI Art. 1);
  - obligations arising out of dealings prior to conclusion of the contract (RI Rec. 10);
  - aspects related to conclusion and validity of contracts where national or Union law lays down form requirements (E Rec. 21).
